const semiCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The band gap decides conductivity', note: 'Quantum mechanics (the solid-state lesson): in crystals, electron energies form bands separated by gaps. Metals overlap (electrons free), insulators gap ~5+ eV (locked), semiconductors gap ~1 eV — promotable by heat, light, or impurity: the tunable middle.' },
    { math: '2 · Doping: engineering conductivity', note: 'Phosphorus (5 valence electrons) in silicon donates a free electron — n-type. Boron (3) leaves a hole that conducts like a positive charge — p-type. One-in-a-million atoms, orders-of-magnitude conductivity: defect engineering (the crystal lesson) as an industry.' },
    { math: '3 · The junction is the device', note: 'p meets n: electrons and holes diffuse across, building a field that blocks further flow — until a forward voltage tips it. The diode (one-way valve) and, with a third terminal to steer the barrier, the transistor: the gate lesson\'s switch, made from a boundary.' },
    { math: '4 · From sand to circuit', note: 'Purify silicon (99.9999999% — "nine nines"), grow single crystals (the defect lesson\'s perfection), slice wafers, pattern with photolithography (light printing the circuit), implant dopants, layer insulators and metal. Billions of transistors per chip: the greatest manufacturing precision in human history (the compilers lesson\'s hardware, made).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/semiconductors-and-doping">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Semiconductors are <strong>materials whose conductivity is a design variable</strong> — the
    band gap supplies the middle ground, doping supplies the control, and the p–n junction turns
    controlled conductivity into logic. Every chip is a materials-science artefact that happens
    to compute.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="band gaps, doping, the junction, the fab chain" widget="equationStep" config={semiCfg} />

  <h2>The engineering frontier</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Moore\'s law was a materials sprint:</strong> each generation required new physics — high-k dielectrics when silicon dioxide thinned to leak, strain engineering to speed carriers, FinFETs then gate-all-around to keep the switch switching. The "law" was materials science delivering on schedule (the solid-state lesson\'s industry).</li>
    <li><strong>Beyond silicon:</strong> GaN and SiC handle higher voltages and temperatures (EV inverters and chargers — the energy lesson\'s efficiency, material by material); 2-D materials (graphene, MoS₂) wait at the research edge of the roadmap.</li>
    <li><strong>LEDs and lasers are band-gap instruments:</strong> photon energy equals the gap — engineer the gap (III–V alloys), engineer the colour; blue LEDs (Nobel 2014, after decades of "impossible") completed white light and repointed the world\'s lighting (the solid-state lesson\'s everyday proof).</li>
    <li><strong>Solar cells are the junction run backwards:</strong> photons promote electrons across the gap, the field separates charges, current flows — the photovoltaic effect is the same p–n device as the diode, harvest mode (the energy lesson\'s cheapest electron, explained by this lesson\'s bands).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the physics ladder\'s solid-state lesson; Sze, Physics of Semiconductor Devices; Nobel 2014 (Akasaki, Amano, Nakamura).</p>
</TopicPage>
